  "notes": "Turin Italo-dance, and the defining parameter the entry names is timbral rather than rhythmic: \"the pitch-shifted / formant-shifted lead vocal — Jeffrey Jey's voice transposed up into a cartoon register as a timbral hook, which prefigures the hyperpop lane by fifteen years\". That is a melody register pushed to 76-99 with a pitched-vocal timbre hint. Two drum facts differentiate it inside the Italo block: \"Italo-dance tom rolls into the chorus\" (a tom/tomHigh perc lane, snare-roll ladder off) and \"a 16th hat drives the verse\" (a fixed 16th base with the fill density up at 0.45). The bass is the Italo signature the entry states — \"octave-jumping syncopated synth bass, bright and short\" — offbeat 8ths with an octave passing tone and a staccato-short envelope implied by the low glide. Harmony is the i-VI-III-VII vamp with a bright chorus, which the research marks `[LOW-CONF]`; that mark is repeated here. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the velocity tiers, the registers and every density and probability — the entry gives instruments and adjectives, and calls the drum and tempo detail genre-derived.",
